From e2a7c1a1ad6ea9f9ebd9ebabd0e66c5f9bf2aefc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JingMatrix <jingmatrix@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 14:33:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 18/47] Re-attest pre-existing keys on config push
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A key an app generated before it was covered by a profile still carries the real
hardware attestation — an unlocked root of trust rooted in the device's real
attestation key. After each config push commits, re-root those keys to the keybox.

The re-sign runs in the TA, reusing the patch-mode path: the daemon hands each
existing leaf to the lib over a new "resign" request on the @teesim channel
(teesim_cfg_resign), and the router calls teesim_km_patch_attestation on the owning
profile's TA — keeping the leaf's public key and attestation content, re-rooting the
issuer and root of trust under the keybox. The daemon writes the returned chain back
with keystore2's updateSubcomponent (addressed by KEY_ID, like deleteKeyById); the
key blob is never touched, so the real hardware key keeps signing.

ReAttest, triggered on the lib's ack, enumerates each target uid's stored attestation
leaves from the keystore2 database (KeystoreDb.attestedKeys) and skips our own keys and
any leaf already rooted in the live keybox — so it is idempotent, and a leaf rooted in a
keybox that is no longer configured is re-signed under the new one, self-healing a
keybox rotation. The legacy keystore interceptor has no patch mode and declines to
re-sign.
